The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of Jesus the Messiah, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. And Isaac was the father of Jacob. And Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
And Judah begot Perez and Zerah of Tamar. And Perez begot Hezron. And Hezron begot Ram.
And Ram begot Amminadab. And Amminadab begot Nahshon. And Nahshon begot Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz, and Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed, and Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of king David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
And Solomon the son of Jedediah, the king of Jerusalem, and Rehoboam the son of Solomon, and Abijah the son of Rehoboam, and Asa the son of Abijah.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Jehoram. Jehoram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
And Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh; and Manasseh was the father of Amon; and Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers. And in that time, the Jews were forced to go to Babylon.
And afterward the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel, the son of Shealtiel, the son of Jeconiah, the son of Josiah, the son of Amon, the son of Manasseh, the son of Hezekiah, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, the son of Jotham, the son of Ahaz, the son of Jotham,
And Azur was father to Sadoc. And Sadoc was father to Achim. And Achim was father to Eliud.
And Elioenai was the father of Eleazar. And Eleazar was the father of Matthan. And Matthan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. And Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
Therefore, there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon. And there were 14 generations from that time to the Messiah. And Jesus Christ was born.
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about: His mother Mary was pledged to be married to Joseph. Before they had any children, Mary found out that she was pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
And her husband, Joseph, was a righteous man. He did not want to expose her to shame before the people. So he decided to divorce her quietly.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary as your wife, for the child who is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son, and you shall call him Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.
And all these things have happened to me, so that what the Lord had said might be fulfilled, for he had said, by the hand of the prophet:
The virgin will conceive a child, and she will give birth to a son, and his name will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not consummate their marriage until she had given birth to a son, and he named him Jesus Joseph.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea during the time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
And they said, "Where is he who has been born king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and all Jerusalem was disturbed with him.
Herod called together all the chief priests of the people. He also sent for the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
They answered, "In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written through the prophet:
"«And as for you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, too small to be among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come one who will be a ruler over Israel, whose origin is from of old, from ancient days.» (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the Magi, and from them he diligently inquired about the time when the star appeared.
And he sent them to Bethlehem, saying, "Go and search diligently for the child, and when you have found him, bring me word, so that I may also go and worship him."
And after the wise men had listened to the king, they continued their journey. And the star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. And it came to rest finally over the place where the child was.
And when they saw the star, they rejoiced exceedingly.
The wise men went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they bowed down to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
And after the wise men had departed, Joseph saw a dream. And in the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him and said to him, "Rise! and take the young child and his mother and flee into Egypt. And remain there until I bring you word to return. Because Herod will seek the young child to destroy him."
Joseph got up and went at night to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
And they remained there until King Herod died. And thus the words spoken by the Lord through the prophet were fulfilled, where he said, "I have surely called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
And Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him, and he became furiously angry. He issued his orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the young boys who were two years old or less were to be killed. This coincided with the time when the wise men saw the star.
And thus the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled, for he had said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, lamentation and great weeping. Rachel weeps for her children, refusing to be comforted, for they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
And after Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream in Egypt, where he was still there. And in the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise and take the child and his mother and go to the land of Israel, for those who sought to kill the child are dead."
Joseph stood and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But afterwards he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This is what made Joseph afraid to go there. Instead, Joseph returned to the region of Galilee after receiving a warning in a dream.
And he lived there in a city called Nazareth. And thus the prophecy spoken by the prophets about Jesus was fulfilled, for they had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way.
And in those days, John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent of your sins, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. For he said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
And some people from Jerusalem and all Judea and the coastal region of the land of Israel, beyond the Jordan, went out to him.
And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bring forth fruit meet for repentance.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Our father is Abraham.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already placed at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repentance for your sins. But one will come after me who is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire."
And the sickle in his hand, he cleans his threshing floor and gathers the wheat into his granary, but the straw he burns with unquenchable fire." The Baptism of Jesus
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so for now, for it is proper to do this, because by doing this we fulfill the holy will of God." John agreed.
And after Jesus was baptized, he immediately went up out of the water. And at that moment the heavens were opened, and Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
And a voice came from heaven, saying, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
The Temptation of Jesus
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert, and there the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
So the experienced one came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city and placed him on the highest point of the temple.
He said to him: "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
And he said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan, for it is written: 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and were serving him. And Jesus began to preach.
And John was put in prison. And when Jesus heard that, he withdrew to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located beside the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled, who said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! O Galilee, where the Gentiles dwell! The land stretching along the Mediterranean Sea! The region east of the Jordan River!
And the people dwelling in darkness have seen a great light; and on those dwelling in the shadow of death, a light has dawned. (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
And from that time, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
Jesus was once walking by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets at once and followed him.
Then he continued on his way and saw two other brothers. They were James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
They left the boat and their father at once and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he was teaching in the synagogues. He was preaching the good news about the kingdom of God. He was healing every disease and illness among the people.
His news spread throughout Syria. And people brought him everyone who was sick with various types of diseases. Some were suffering from intense pain. And demons were controlling others. And some were trembling violently. And others were not able to move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
And great multitudes followed him. For some people came from Galilee, from the region which is called Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region beyond the Jordan.
Jesus bestows blessings.
And Jesus saw the multitudes, so he went up into the mountain. And he sat down, and his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. And he said:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for the sake of doing good. For the Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you for my sake. Blessed are you also when they say all manner of evil against you falsely for my sake.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward in heaven is great. This is how people mistreated the prophets who lived in ancient times. Salt and Light
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled under people's feet.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather puts it on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.
Thus, people do not light a lamp and put it under a basket, but rather they put it on a lampstand, and it provides light for everyone in the house.
"Similarly, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven. Jesus gave a perfect meaning to the law."
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish but to fulfill.
What I am about to tell you is a fact. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not the smallest letter or stroke of the pen will disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.
Do not neglect even the smallest of these commandments, and do not teach others to do so. For if you do, you will be considered the least important among those in the Kingdom of Heaven. Rather, practice these commandments and teach them. Then you will be considered important in the Kingdom of Heaven."
This is what I tell you: You must be even more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. Otherwise, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
You have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13). Whoever commits murder will be held accountable for it.
But I tell you this: do not be angry with your brother; anyone who is angry with their brother will be brought to judgment. And anyone who says to their brother, 'You good-for-nothing,' will be brought to judgment. But anyone who says, 'You fool,' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Let us suppose that you offer your gift at the altar and that you remember there that your brother has something against you.
Place your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Let's suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. If you do not do that, he may hand you over to the judge. And the judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is true. You will not leave until you have paid your last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But I say to you: whoever looks at a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
And if your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away from you. For it is better for you to lose one of your members than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
"And if your right hand causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. For it is more profitable for you that one of your members perish, rather than that your whole body go into hell. Divorce."
It was said, 'Suppose a man divorces his wife. If he does so, he must give her a certificate of divorce.' (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: anyone who divorces his wife forces her to become an adulteress. And anyone who marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man cannot divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Oaths
And again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. For they were told, 'Do not break the covenants that you made with the Lord. Keep the oaths that you swore to Him.'"
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make any promises like that at all. Don't make them in the name of heaven. For that is God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the Earth. For this is the seat on which the feet of God rest. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. For this is the city of the great King.
And do not swear by your head; for you cannot make even a single hair white or black.
Let the word 'yes' mean only 'yes'. And let the word 'no' mean 'no'. Anything more than that is from the evil one. Be kind to others.
Have you not heard that it was said, 'An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth'? (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21).
But this is what I tell you: Do not resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, turn to him your left cheek also.
Assume someone is suing you for your shirt. Let him take your coat too.
Assume someone is forcing you to walk one mile. Go with them for two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not turn away from someone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'Love your neighbor' (Leviticus 19:18) and 'hate your enemy.'
But this is what I tell you: love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.
Then you will be the sons of your Father in heaven. "For he makes his sun rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
"And if you love those who love you, what reward do you deserve? Even tax collectors do that."
If you only submit to those you know, then what is your superiority over others? Even the unbelievers do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Providing assistance to people in need.
Beware of doing your charitable deeds before men, to be seen by them. Otherwise you will have no reward from your Father who is in heaven.
And when you give to those in need, do not announce it with trumpets, as the hypocrites do in their synagogues and on the streets, so that they may be honored by others. I tell you the truth: they have received their full reward."
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your charity will be secret. Your Father, who sees what you do in secret, will reward you. Prayer
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by men. Verily I say unto you, they have received their reward in full."
And when you pray, go into your room, and close the door and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees what is done in secret will reward you. Your Father knows what you do in secret.
And when you pray, do not babble like the pagans, who think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Do not be like them. Your Father knows what you need even before you ask Him.
Thus you should pray: 'Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from the occasion of sin when we are tempted. Save us from the evil one.
Forgive men when they sin against you. For if you do this, your heavenly Father will also forgive you your sins.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
And when you do not eat, do not be gloomy like the hypocrites, for they change their faces to show themselves to people as mourners, so that they may see people that they are fasting. But I tell you the truth: they have received their reward in full."
But when you abstain from food, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your father, who is unseen, will know. And he will reward you. Your father sees what is done in secret. Save up treasures for yourselves in heaven
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is the lamp of the body. If your eye is good, your whole body will be filled with light.
But what if your eyes are bad? Then darkness will come over the whole body. If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
You cannot serve both God and money. Do not worry saying
"I tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life, about what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, about what you will wear. Is not life more than food? And is not the body more than clothing?"
"Consider the birds of the air; for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they?"
Can you add even one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at how the wild flowers grow. They do not spin or weave.
But that's what I'm telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like these lilies.
And if God clothes the wild grass in such a way, will He not much more clothe you? For the grass is here today and tomorrow is thrown into the fire. Your faith is very weak!
Therefore do not be anxious, saying, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
The wicked are hungry for all these things. Your Father in heaven knows that you need them.
But first seek the kingdom of God, and do his will, and all these things will be given you as well.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow, because tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when judging others.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard by which you judge others. You will be evaluated by the same standard by which you evaluate others.
You look at the sawdust in your friend's eye, but you don't notice the piece of wood in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me take the tiny piece of wood out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the splinter out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before the swine. Otherwise, they may trample them under their feet. Then they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and it will be opened to you.
He who asks shall receive what he wants. He who seeks will find. The door will open for the one who knocks.
Let's assume your son asked for bread. Which one of you would give him a stone?
Or let us suppose that he asked for a fish; which of you would give him a snake?
Although you are evil, you know how to give your children good gifts. How much more, then, will the Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
Do to others in everything what you would want them to do to you. This is what is written in the law and in the words of the prophets. The great and small gates.
Go through the narrow gate into God's kingdom. For the gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to destruction and the fire. Many are those who go down that road.
But the gate is narrow and the way leading to life is difficult, and few are those who find it. The Tree and Its Fruit
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know them by what they do. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny patches?"
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and did we not cast out demons in your name, and did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I clearly say to them: "I never knew you! Away from me, you doers of evil!" The wise and the foolish builders.
Everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
Rain falls. Water rises. Winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears these words of mine and does not act on them will be like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
Rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud noise.
And when Jesus had finished all these sayings, the crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority, and he did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain, and a great crowd followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me 'clean'".
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I want to do this. Be clean!" And the man was immediately healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. You will be a testimony to them." A Roman Centurion Believes
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman officer came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
And he said, "O Lord, my servant is lying in the house, unable to move, and he is suffering severely."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The commander answered, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you enter my home, but just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
And I myself am a man who is subject to authority, and I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. And I say to that one, "Come," and he comes. And I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it."
And when Jesus heard that, he was amazed, and said to those who followed him, "I tell you the truth, I have not found such great faith, even in Israel."
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven, and will sit down with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
As for those who think that they are the owners, they will be expelled to the outside in the darkness, where they will cry and grind their teeth in a grating noise.
Then Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! It will happen as you believed it would happen." And at that very hour his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her with his hand, and the fever left her. She got up and began to serve him.
And when evening came, they brought to Him many who had demons; and He cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick.
He did this to make what the prophet Isaiah said come true. He said, "He has taken on the suffering that we should have had. He has carried the diseases that should have been ours." (Isaiah 53:4) Following Jesus requires sacrifices.
And Jesus saw the multitudes around him, so he commanded his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the teachers of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "Foxes have dens and birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
And another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me, and let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus went up into a boat. His disciples followed him.
And suddenly a fierce storm came upon the lake, and the waves began to beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
So the disciples went and woke him up, saying, "Lord, save us! We are perishing!"
He answered them, "It is because of your little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they became perfectly calm.
The students were amazed. They asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus healed two men who were under the control of demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, to the region of the Gadarenes. He met two men who were controlled by demons, and they were coming from the tombs. The two men were very fierce, so much so that no one could pass by from there.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come here to punish us before it is time for our trial?"
Not far away, a large group of pigs was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you are going to drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The herd of pigs, about two thousand in number, rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
So those who had been tending the pigs fled and went into the city and told everything and told the people what happened to the men who were possessed by the evil spirits.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus went up into a boat. And he crossed over to the other side of the lake and came to his own city.
Some men brought to him a man who could not walk. The man was lying on a mat. Seeing that they had faith, Jesus said to the man, "Do not be afraid, son, your sins are forgiven."
Then one of my teachers of the religious law said to himself: 'This man speaks words that are very wicked!'
And Jesus knew what they were thinking, and said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Which is easier, to tell the sick person, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then he told the man who could not walk, "Get up, take your mat and go home!"
The man got up and went home.
And when the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. And they praised God, for he had given human beings this kind of authority. Jesus Chooses Matthew
Jesus went on from there, and saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And rising up, Matthew followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
And seeing this, the Pharisees asked his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
And Jesus heard of it, and said: "They that are whole have no need of the physician, but they that are sick have need of him."
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice' (Hosea 6: 6). I did not come to make those who believe they are right with God to follow me. I came to make sinners follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
And one day, the disciples of John came. And they said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, so why do not your disciples fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them; then they will fast.
People do not patch old clothes with a new piece of cloth, because the new piece will separate from the old, thus making the tear even wider.
A dead girl and a suffering woman.
And while Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus got up and went with him, and so did his disciples.
And at that time, a woman approached Jesus from behind. She had suffered from a disease that caused her to bleed blood. This disease had lasted for 12 years. And she touched the edge of his clothes.
And she said to herself, "It will be enough for me to touch his clothes in order to be healed."
Jesus turned and saw her. He said to her, "Woman, do not lose heart." "Your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed at that very moment.
And when Jesus entered the chief priest's house, he saw there the players of flutes. And he saw the noisy crowd.
And he said, "Go, for the young woman is not dead, but is sleeping." And they laughed at him.
And after he had put the crowd outside, he went in and took her by the hand, and the girl arose.
The news spread throughout the region about what Jesus had done. Jesus heals two blind men.
And Jesus went away from there, and two blind men followed Him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
And when Jesus entered the house, the two blind men came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" And they answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he wiped their eyes and said, "Go, eat from what Allah has provided for you." And what you believed in, those are the ones in whose faith we believe for you.
And they could see again. And Jesus warned them severely, saying, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. And they talked about it all over that region.
And while they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. He had a demon controlling him, and he could not speak.
And when the evil spirit came out, the man spoke. And the crowd was amazed. And they said, "We have never seen anything like this in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few workers.
Jesus went to all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, and preached the good news of the kingdom. He healed every disease and affliction.
And when he saw the crowds, he was deeply concerned for them, for they were weary and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is abundant, but the laborers are few.
Ask the Lord of the harvest, therefore, to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus sent out the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to drive out demons and to heal every disease and illness.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Following them are Philip and Bartholomew, as well as Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. There are two others: James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two were Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas was the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following instructions: "Do not go among the Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any town of the Samaritans."
Go rather to the house of Israel, for they are like lost sheep.
And as you go, preach this message: "The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
Heal the sick, raise the dead, make those with skin diseases "clean" again, and drive out demons. You received gifts freely; give freely.
Do not carry gold or silver or copper in your belts.
Do not take a travel bag. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker must be given what they need.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone willing to receive you. And stay at that person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If that house welcomes you, give it the blessing of peace. But if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. And if they do not do so, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that city.
What I am going to tell you is a fact. On the Day of Judgment, the fate of Sodom and Gomorrah will be easier than the fate of that city.
"I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and innocent as doves."
Beware! You will be handed over to local courts, and you will be flogged in their synagogues.
You will be presented to rulers and kings for my sake. And you will be witnesses for them and for the non-Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it, for at that time you will be given the appropriate words to speak.
It will not be you who speaks, but the Spirit of your Father who will speak within you.
The brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed, and the fathers their sons. And sons will rise up against their parents and cause their death.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But the one who endures to the end, that one will be saved.
"When people persecute you in one place, flee to another. What I tell you is true: you will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes."
The student is not better than his teacher. And the servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for a disciple to be like his master, and enough for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, how much more will the members of his household be called by that name?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed, and what is hidden will be brought to light.
What I tell you in the dark, say in the light. And what is whispered in your ear, proclaim from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and after that have no more that they can do. But I will show you whom you should fear: Fear him who, after he has killed, has authority to cast into hell. Yes, I tell you, fear him.
" أليست عصفوران يباعان بفلس واحد؟ ومع ذلك لا يسقط واحد منهما على الأرض دون أن يعلم أبوكم بذلك."
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Therefore do not be anxious, for you are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about the person who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about the person who says in front of others that he doesn't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make "sons against their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. And wives will be against their in-laws.
"The enemies of man are the people of his house" (Micah 7:6).
Whoever loves his father or his mother more than me does not deserve me. And whoever loves his son or his daughter more than me does not deserve me.
Whoever does not bear their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
He who receives you receives me, and he who receives me receives Him who sent me.
Let's suppose that someone welcomes a prophet as a prophet. Then that person will receive the reward of the prophet. And let's suppose that someone welcomes a righteous man as a righteous man. Then that person will receive the reward of the righteous man.
Let's suppose that someone offered, even if it was just a glass of cold water, to a young child following me. What I am going to tell you now is a fact. This person will definitely be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. Then he went to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was supposed to come? Or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered them, "Go and tell John what you hear and see.
The blind receive their sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. The poor have good news preached to them.
Blessed are those who do not leave their faith for my sake.
And as John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swayed by the wind?"
Otherwise, what have you gone out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. The people who dress in fine clothes live in the palaces of kings.
So what did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the Holy Book. It says: "I will send my messenger before you. And he will prepare the way for you" (Malachi 3:1).
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one more important than John the Baptist has ever been born. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
For days, even from the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and powerful people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Those who have ears should listen.
How shall I liken the people of this time? They are like children sitting in the markets, calling out to the people. They say:
We played for you on the flute, but you did not dance. We sang a funeral song, but you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, and people said, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank with you. And they say, 'This man is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is justified by her deeds.' The cities that did not repent.
And Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles, because the people there had not repented of their sins. And He said:
"Woe to you, Korazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes."
But I tell you this: it will be better for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you.
"And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No! But you will be brought down to Hades. For if the miracles had been performed in Sodom that had been done in you, it would have remained to this day."
But I tell you this: it will be more tolerable for Sodom in the day of judgment than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
And Jesus said at that time, "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children.
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to introduce to him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be my servants and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was passing through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to pluck the heads of wheat and eat them.
And seeing this, the Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. It is not lawful to do any work on the Sabbath." But your disciples do that!»
Jesus answered them, "Haven't you read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to partake of it.
Have you not read the law? It mentions how the priests in the temple are to do their work on every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that he who is greater than the temple is here.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6: 6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Then he went out from there and entered their synagogue.
And there was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus, so they asked him, "Is it lawful for us to heal on the Sabbath?"
And he said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not reach in and pull it out?"
A man is more valuable than sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath."
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, just like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus was aware of all the plans of the Pharisees. Therefore, He left that place. Many followed Him, and He healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell anyone who he was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet Isaiah, who said:
This is my servant whom I have chosen, the one who is pleasing to me. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will bring the nations to tell them that everything will be alright.
He will not argue or shout. And no one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bending branch. He will not extinguish a faintly burning flame. He will fix everything.
You said, 'I will place my nations' hope in him.' (Isaiah 42: 1-4). Jesus and Beelzebul.
A man controlled by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man was able to speak and see.
All the people were amazed and were saying, "Can this be the Son of David?"
And the Pharisees heard this, and they said, 'This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons.'
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan drives out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul, so by whose power do your followers cast out demons? For they will then be your judges.
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about this matter. How can you enter the house of a strong man and take only what the man has? You must first restrain him. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
So, this is what I tell you: People will be forgiven for every sin and every blasphemy spoken against God. But whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. And no one who speaks like this will be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you make the tree good, then its fruits will be good. And if you make the tree bad, then its fruits will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruits.
"O nest of poisonous vipers! How is it possible for you, who are evil, to say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts."
A good man says good things. These things come from the goodness he puts inside himself. An evil man says evil things. These things come from the evil he puts inside himself.
But that's what I'm telling you. On the day of reckoning, people will have to answer for every careless word they spoke.
You will be condemned or acquitted based on your words. The sign of Yunus the miracle.
And some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
And he answered: "The evil and unbelieving people seek a sign, but no sign will be given to them except the sign of the prophet Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a huge fish for three days and three nights. And so will the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth for three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with the people who are living now. And the Ninevites will prove that these people are guilty. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now, someone more important than Jonah is here.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with the people who are now living, and will condemn them. She came from a very great distance to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now, there is one here who is greater than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It passes through dry areas, searching for a place to rest. But it does not find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned, swept, and put in order.
Then the evil spirit returns and takes with it seven other spirits more wicked than it, and they enter and reside there. The condition of that man becomes worse than before. This is how the condition of the wicked will be in this age». The Mother of Jesus and His Brothers
And while Jesus was still speaking to the crowds, behold, His mother and His brothers and sisters were standing outside, seeking to speak to Him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak with you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "This is my mother! And these are my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
And on that day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
A great crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things by way of parables. And he said, "The sower went out to sow his seed."
Scattering the seeds on the ground, some fell on the road, and the birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
The other seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
And as for the other seeds, they fell into good soil and produced a crop a hundred times, or sixty times, or thirty times, greater than the planted seed.
Let him who has ears listen.
The students came to him. And they asked him: "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This opportunity has not been given to outsiders."
Everyone who has this kind of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have far too much. As for those who do not have this kind of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away from them.
And that is why I use stories when I talk to people. I say, "They look, but they do not really see. They listen, but they do not really hear or understand."
In them is fulfilled the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah, who said: "You will indeed hear but never understand; you will indeed see but never perceive."
Their hearts are hardened; they barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they would have seen with their eyes. They would have heard with their ears. They would have understood with their hearts. They would have turned back to the Lord, and then he would have healed them." (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But woe to you scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widow's houses, and for a pretense you make long prayer. Therefore you will receive greater condemnation.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. Many prophets and righteous people have wanted to see what you are seeing. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you are hearing. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He snatches away what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
And others received the seeds that fell on the rocks. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it joyfully.
But they have no roots. And so they last only for a little while. For when trouble or persecution comes because of the message, they quickly fall away from their faith.
And others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message, but the worries of this life and the false promises of riches choke it. So it does not bear fruit.
But others received the seeds that fell on good soil. They are those who hear the message and understand it. They produce a harvest more than the farmer sowed by 100, 60, or 30 times." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another story to the crowds and said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven: A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
So, 'Ubayd, the owner of the field, came to him and said to him: "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where have these weeds come from?"
He answered, "It was an enemy who did this." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner answered. "For while you are pulling up the weeds, you may also root up the wheat."
Let both grow together until the harvest. Then I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them: Gather the weeds first. Tie them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it into my storage." The Parables of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast
Jesus told another parable to the crowds, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest of all the garden plants. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast, which a woman took and mixed with a large amount of flour, and the dough became leavened."
Jesus was addressing the multitudes with all these things, using parables. And he did not say anything to them without a parable.
And so the words spoken by the prophet were fulfilled. For he had said, "I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter hidden things from the foundation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explained the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. And His disciples came to Him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. And the good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. But the tares are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are being pulled out and burned in the fire. This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather from his kingdom all causes of sin, and those who do evil will be cast out.
They cast them into the blazing, scorching hellfire. There they weep and gnash their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Tales of the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl.
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
He found one of great value. So he went and sold everything he owned. And he bought that pearl. The Story of the Net.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the lake, and it gathered every kind of fish.
And when it was full, the fishermen drew it up on the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But the bad fish they threw away.
Thus it will be on the Day of Resurrection. The angels will come. And they will separate those who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They throw the criminals into the burning fire, and they cry out in it and grit their teeth.
And Jesus said to them, "Do you understand all these things?" They said to Him, "Yes."
He said to them, "To what can I compare the people of this generation? They are like children sitting in the marketplace. One calls out to another, saying, 'We played the flute for you, but you did not dance. We sang a mournful song, but you did not weep.' For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, 'He has a demon.' The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and you say, 'Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is justified by her deeds." A Prophet Without Honor
And when Jesus had finished these parables, he departed from there.
And he came to his hometown Nazareth. And there he began to teach the people in their synagogue. And they were amazed. And they wondered: "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?"
"Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother called Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?"
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country, and among his own kin."
And He did few miracles there because of their unbelief.
John the Baptist was beheaded.
And at that time Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
And he said to his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! Therefore, he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John. He bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod Philip's brother.
John was saying to Herod, "You have no right to marry her, because it is against the law."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. And Herod was very pleased with her.
He swore and promised her that he would give her anything she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very upset. But he thought of his promise and his guests whom he had invited for dinner. So he asked one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
And his head was brought on a large plate and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
And John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people
Jesus heard what had happened to John the Baptist, and he wanted to be alone by himself. So he got into a boat and went to a quiet place. But the crowds heard about it, and they followed him on foot from their towns.
And when Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. So he healed their sick.
And when evening came, his disciples came to him and said, "This place is deserted, and the hour is already late. Send the crowds away, so they can go into the villages and buy for themselves food."
Jesus answered them, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. Then He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to His disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
And they all ate and were satisfied. And the disciples gathered twelve baskets of leftover fragments.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
And Jesus immediately instructed his disciples to get into the boat and go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, and then he dismissed the crowd.
And after he had sent them away, he went up into the mountain to pray by himself. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far out to sea. Waves were hitting it because the wind was against it.
Jesus went out to the disciples early in the morning. And he walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake, so they were afraid. They said, "He is a ghost!" and they screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately called out to them, saying, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid!"
"Lord, is it you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." Peter got out of the boat and, walking on the water, he came toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus immediately stretched out his hand, grasped him, and said to him, "O man of little faith, why did you doubt?"
And when they got into the boat, the wind ceased.
Then he worshipped those who had been in the ark with him, Jesus. And they said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
And they crossed the lake and came to the land of Ginnessart.
And the men who lived there knew Jesus. So they sent messengers to the country around and brought all the sick to Jesus.
And they asked him to allow the sick to touch only the edge of his cloak. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
And some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus, and they asked him:
Why do your disciples not live according to the teachings of the elders? Your disciples do not wash their hands before eating!"
Jesus replied, "Why do you not obey the commandments of God? Instead, you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will surely be put to death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they are not obligated to honor their parents with their gift. You nullify the word of God so that you can follow your own teachings.
"O ye hypocrites! It was Isaiah the prophet who was right when he prophesied about you, for he said:
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They only teach human rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him and said, "Hear and understand.
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to him and said, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard these words?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant, and they will be pulled up by the roots."
Let them alone. The Pharisees and Sadducees are hypocrites and blind guides. If the blind lead the blind, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Don't you understand even now?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes into the stomach, then it is expelled from the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also, theft, false testimony, and lying.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." The faith of a Canaanite woman.
And Jesus left the region of Galilee and went to the regions of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman from Canaan who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is demon-possessed and suffers terribly."
And Jesus did not say a word. But his disciples came to him and begged him, saying, "Send her away, because she is following us and crying out."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. And she said, "Oh Lord! Help me!"
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Yes, sir," she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you want." And her daughter was healed from that very hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus departed from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the mountain and sat down.
Great multitudes came to him, bringing with them those who were lame, blind, mute, or those who had any other kind of disease. They laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The paralyzed were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. And the people glorified the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him, and he said, "I feel deeply concerned about these people. They have been with me for three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, otherwise they will become very weak on their way home."
His disciples answered him, "We have no more than five loaves and two fish here, unless we are expected to go and buy food for this huge crowd."
Jesus asked them, "How many loaves do you have?" They answered, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the multitudes to sit down on the ground.
So he took the seven loaves and the fish, and he gave thanks, and then he broke them and gave them to his disciples. And the disciples distributed the food to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples picked up seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
And after Jesus had sent the crowd away, he went up on the mountain and sat down. And the disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to them in parables?" He answered them, "To you, it has been given to know the secrets of the kingdom of heaven; but to them, it has not been given. For whoever has, to him more will be given, and he will have abundance; but whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away from him. Therefore I speak to them in parables, because while seeing, they do not see, and while hearing, they do not hear, nor do they understand. And in them the prophecy of Isaiah is fulfilled, which says, 'In the very act of listening, they will not understand; and in the very act of seeing, they will not perceive; for the heart of this people has become dull, and their ears are hard of hearing, and their eyes they have closed; lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and should turn for me to heal them.' "But as for these in the parable, the evil one sowed the weeds among the wheat, but he went away. And when the wheat sprouted and formed grain, then the weeds also appeared. And the servants of the householder came and said to him, 'Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? How then does it have weeds?' He said to them, 'An enemy has done this.' And the servants said to him, 'Do you want us to go and gather them?' But he said, 'No; lest while you gather the weeds, you uproot the wheat along with them. Let both grow together until the harvest; and at the time of the harvest I will say to the reapers, "Gather the weeds first, and bind them in bundles to be burned; but gather the wheat into my barn." ' "Then Jesus sent the crowd away and went into the house. And his disciples came to him and asked, 'Do you not understand that I am not talking to you people?' "Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, 'I assure you, unless a grain of wheat falls to the ground and dies, it remains just a single grain. But if it dies, it produces much grain. The man who loves his life will lose it; the man who hates his life in this world will keep it for eternal life. If any man serves me, let him follow me; and where I am, there will my servant be also. If anyone serves me, my Father will honor him.' "Then Jesus said to his disciples, 'Truly I tell you, it is hard for someone who is wealthy to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.' "And when Jesus heard this, he said to them, 'Children, how hard it is to enter the kingdom of heaven! It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.' "And they were astonished at his words. But Jesus said to them, 'Children, don't let these words be a stumbling block to you. For truly I tell you, anyone who does not receive the kingdom of heaven like a child will certainly not enter it.' "And Jesus called a little child to him, and he stood the child in the midst of them. And he said, 'I assure you, unless you repent and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Whoever then humbles himself like this child, he is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven. Whoever receives one such child in my name receives me; and whoever receives me receives him who sent me.' "And Jesus also said to his disciples, 'There is nothing hidden that will not be revealed, nor is anything secret that will not be known. You have been told all this so that you may not fall.' "And Jesus also said to the crowds, 'Pay attention to what you hear. With the measure you use, it will be measured to you—and even more. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.' "And Jesus also said to the crowds, 'Beware of the
Jesus was asked to provide miraculous proof.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven, demonstrating his miraculous power.
He replied, "In the evening you look at the sky. And you say, 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
