"For a wicked and adulterous generation seeks after a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of the prophet Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. And they had forgotten to bring bread with them.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out and beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
And the disciples talked about this matter among themselves. And they said, "He must have said this because we did not bring bread with us."
And Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "O little faith, why are you discussing among yourselves that you have no bread?"
Don't you understand yet? Remember the five loaves that fed five thousand people? Remember how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand people? Do you not remember how many baskets you filled with the fragments?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was not asking them to beware of the leaven used in bread, but He was warning them about what was being taught by the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "John the Baptist." Some said, "Elijah." Others said, "Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Then Simon Peter answered him, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
This is what I say to you: You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of Hell will not be strong enough to destroy them.
I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
And from that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem, and there he would suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and then he would rise from the dead on the third day.
And Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "No, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself. And let him take up his cross and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What profit does a man gain if he should win the whole world and lose his own soul? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and His angels will come with Him. He will repay each person according to what they have done.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of the people standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom."
Jesus' appearance changed when he appeared.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and brought them up to a high mountain. And there they were all alone.
And behold, before them His form was transformed. And His face shone like the sun, and His clothes became white as light.
And behold, Moses and Elijah appeared to them. And Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelters. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
And when the disciples heard this, they were afraid and fell on their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them, and said, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
And when they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
And they went down from the mountain. And while they were going down, Jesus told them what they must do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
And his disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "This is true. It is supposed to be Elijah who comes and makes everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him, but they did to him whatever they pleased. So also will they do to the Son of Man."
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
And as they approached the crowd, a man came to Jesus. And he knelt down before him.
He said, "Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is shaking violently and suffering greatly, and he often falls into the fire or into the water."
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
Jesus answered, "You unbelieving and wicked generation! How long shall I be with you and bear with you? Bring the boy here to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. And immediately, the boy was healed.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why could we not cast out that demon?"
He answered, "Because your faith is too weak. What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
